   195	When working with an `adder` method and JPA entities, Mapstruct assumes that the target collections are initialized with a collection implementation (e.g. an `ArrayList`). You can use factories to create a new target entity with initialized collections instead of Mapstruct creating the target entity by its constructor.
   196	====
   197	
   198	[[implementation-types-for-collection-mappings]]
   199	=== Implementation types used for collection mappings
   200	
   201	When an iterable or map mapping method declares an interface type as return type, one of its implementation types will be instantiated in the generated code. The following table shows the supported interface types and their corresponding implementation types as instantiated in the generated code:
   202	
   203	.Collection mapping implementation types
   204	|===
   205	|Interface type|Implementation type
   206	
   207	|`Iterable`|`ArrayList`
   208	
   209	|`Collection`|`ArrayList`
   210	
   211	|`List`|`ArrayList`
   212	
   213	|`Set`|`LinkedHashSet`
   214	
   215	|`SequencedSet`|`LinkedHashSet`
   216	
   217	|`SortedSet`|`TreeSet`
   218	
   219	|`NavigableSet`|`TreeSet`
   220	
   221	|`Map`|`LinkedHashMap`
   222	
   223	|`SequencedMap`|`LinkedHashMap`
   224	
   225	|`SortedMap`|`TreeMap`
   226	
   227	|`NavigableMap`|`TreeMap`
   228	
   229	|`ConcurrentMap`|`ConcurrentHashMap`
   230	|`ConcurrentNavigableMap`|`ConcurrentSkipListMap`
   231	|===
   232	
   233	[[configurable-collection-implementations]]
   234	==== Configuring collection implementation types
   235	
   236	The built-in implementation for a supported interface can be replaced in a Java properties resource on the mapping
   237	compilation classpath. By default, MapStruct reads `mapstruct.properties` from the classpath root when it exists. The
   238	`mapstruct.configurationFile` annotation processor option selects a different resource by relative path. The resource
   239	is read from the mapping compilation classpath, not from the annotation processor's own classloader.
   240	
   241	Each replacement uses the interface's qualified name as part of the property key:
   242	
   243	[source, properties]
   244	----
   245	mapstruct.collectionImplementation.java.util.List=com.example.CustomList
   246	mapstruct.collectionImplementation.java.util.Map=com.example.CustomMap
   247	----
   248	
   249	The implementation must be a publicly accessible concrete class assignable to the configured interface. It must have
   250	the same number of unbounded type parameters, preserve those parameters when implementing the interface, and expose a
   251	public no-argument constructor that declares no checked exceptions. If it also has a public `int` constructor that
   252	declares no checked exceptions, MapStruct passes the source collection or map size as its initial capacity when that
   253	size is available.
   254	
   255	The configured implementation is used whenever MapStruct constructs that exact supported interface result, including
   256	bean property copies and results materialized from streams. An applicable object factory still takes precedence.
